## Review

The diff removes the `?` from `milliseconds?` in the parsing regex, so the alternation now only accepts the plural `milliseconds` and no longer matches the singular `millisecond`.

This is not a dead branch: the `switch` in `parse` explicitly handles `case 'millisecond':`, and in the original version a string like `'1millisecond'` matched the regex (number `1`, unit `millisecond`), lowered to `millisecond`, and returned `1`. After the patch, no alternative can consume `millisecond` (the anchor `$` forbids a partial match), so the regex fails and `parse` returns `undefined`. The same applies to `'5millisecond'`, `'-1millisecond'`, and case variants like `'1MilliSecond'`.

The library's own tests only cover the plural form (e.g. `'100 milliseconds'`), which is why the suite stays green — this is exactly the kind of gap the unit tests don't catch. Every other alternative in the regex (`msecs?`, `seconds?`, etc.) is untouched, so the singular `millisecond` is the sole regression.

```json
{"defect": true, "testFile": {"path": "proof-test.js", "content": "var assert = require('assert');
var ms = require('./');

describe('proof', function () {
  it('still parses the singular "millisecond" unit', function () {
    assert.strictEqual(ms('1millisecond'), 1);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('5millisecond'), 5);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('-1millisecond'), -1);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('100millisecond'), 100);
  });
});
"}, "note": "Dropping the ? from 'milliseconds?' makes the regex reject the singular unit 'millisecond', which the switch statement still handles and which previously parsed (e.g. ms('1millisecond') === 1). The patch now returns undefined for such inputs, a caller-observable regression the existing plural-only tests don't cover."}
```

